  "knowledgePattern": "Split parameter signs and count the union without duplicates",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Separate the sign of p",
      "body": "<p>Check p<0 and p=0 in the original equation before treating p>0.</p>",
      "recap": "An absolute value is never negative.",
      "pitfall": "The factor p³ outside the right-hand modulus can be negative.",
      "pause": "Record what equality would require in each of the first two cases.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l09",
          "reminder": "Split into cases that cover every permitted parameter value."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Remove the moduli carefully",
      "body": "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> only when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. Apply this to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Equal absolute values allow equal or opposite expressions.",
      "pitfall": "Do not keep just the equal-sign equation.",
      "pause": "Write and simplify the two polynomial equations.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-function-mapping",
          "reminder": "An absolute value keeps size but removes the sign."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Count roots and check repeats",
      "body": "<p>Factor one polynomial. For the other, write its value at v minus its value at u, where v&gt;u, and factor the difference. This helps you decide whether it always increases.</p>",
      "recap": "A shared root must be counted only once.",
      "pitfall": "Several roots of an equation need not give the same number of distinct intersections after combining cases.",
      "pause": "Count the roots in both equations, check when a root belongs to both, and complete the cases for p.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b3-l08",
          "reminder": "Combine roots from separate equations, removing overlaps."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the right side is nonpositive and the left side nonnegative. Equality would require both sides zero, forcing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> simultaneously, impossible. There are no intersections. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the equation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so there is one.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. The equation is the union of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The first factors as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, giving three distinct roots <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The second is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. It is strictly increasing, since for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The second cubic has exactly one root: its signs at 0 and 2 are opposite, so a root exists, and strict increase proves uniqueness. Its root coincides with one from the first equation only when both original signed equations hold, requiring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>5.</strong> Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> gives four intersections, while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> gives three because one root is shared. Altogether the attainable counts are 0,1,3,4, so 2 cannot occur. Answer E. This proof covers every allowed parameter, rather than relying on a few sketches.</p>"
}
